.TH OTPME-POLICY 1 "2026-10-26" "OTPme 0.3.0a287" "OTPme Manual"
.SH NAME
otpme-policy \- manage policies: password rules, login times, defaults for new objects and more
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B otpme-policy
[\fB\-\-type\fR \fIpolicy_type\fR]
.I command
[\fIoptions\fR] [\fIpolicy\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.na
.nh
.B otpme-policy
adds, configures and removes policies.
A policy is an object in a unit.
It does nothing until it is assigned to another object, with \fBadd_policy\fR of that object's command, e.g. \fBotpme\-user add_policy\fR.
Then it acts at its moments: when a password is set, at a login, when an object is added to a unit, before an action.
.PP
What a policy does depends on its type, see \fBPOLICY TYPES\fR.
An object takes one policy of each type.
A new site comes with a set of them, see \fBWhat a new site has\fR.
.hy
.ad
.SH POLICY TYPES
The type is chosen when the policy is added (\fB\-\-type\fR) and cannot be changed.
An object takes one policy of each type; a second one of the same type is refused.
What the settings of a type are is under \fBPOLICY TYPE DETAILS\fR.
.TP
.B password
How good a password or PIN has to be.
For user, token, host, node; a token without one is checked with the one of its user.
.TP
.B logintimes
When a login is allowed.
For user, token, role, host, node, client, accessgroup.
.TP
.B autodisable
Disable the object it is assigned to after a time, or after it was unused for that long.
For every object type except the realm, policies, scripts and passwords, and not for an object the realm or a site comes with, e.g. the admin user.
"Unused for" only where the object keeps its last use: user, token, host, node, client, device, resolver, share.
.TP
.B forcetoken
Which token types or pass types a login there must use.
For role, host, node, client, accessgroup.
.TP
.B authonaction
Log in again before an action, e.g. changing a PIN.
For every object type.
.TP
.B defaultunits
Which unit a new object goes to, for the objects a user adds.
For user.
.TP
.B defaultgroups
Which groups a new user joins, and their default group.
For unit.
.TP
.B defaultroles
Which roles the default token of a new user joins.
For unit.
.TP
.B defaultpolicies
Which policies a new object gets, per object type.
For site, unit: the unit or site the object is added to.
.TP
.B idrange
Which uidNumber and gidNumber a new user or group gets.
For site, unit.
.TP
.B tokenacls
Which ACLs a new token gets, and the user and token that added it.
For user.
.TP
.B objecttemplates
Which template a new user or host is made from.
For unit.
.SS What a new site has
A site is created with these policies:
.TP
.B auth_on_action
authonaction, on every object.
.TP
.B password_strength
password, on every user, token, host and node.
.TP
.B token_acls
tokenacls, on every user.
.TP
.B default_units
defaultunits, on every user.
.TP
.B id_range
idrange, on the site.
.TP
.B default_policies
defaultpolicies, on the site and on every unit.
It is what hands the ones above to new objects.
.TP
\fBdefault_groups\fR, \fBdefault_roles\fR
defaultgroups and defaultroles, on the unit \fBusers\fR.
.TP
.B object_templates
objecttemplates, on every unit.
.TP
\fBauto_disable_hour\fR, \fB_day\fR, \fB_week\fR, \fB_month\fR, \fB_year\fR
autodisable, assigned to nothing.
.TP
\fBworkhours_login\fR, \fBweekend_login\fR
logintimes, assigned to nothing.
.TP
\fBforce_token_otp\fR, \fBforce_token_non_static\fR
forcetoken, assigned to nothing.
.SH COMMANDS
.SS Policy Management
.TP
.B add \fIpolicy\fR
Create a policy.
The type is given with \fB\-\-type\fR, e.g. \fBotpme-policy \-\-type password add pw\-default\fR.
See \fBPOLICY TYPES\fR for the types.
.br
A policy goes to the unit \fBpolicies\fR, or to the one a \fBdefaultunits\fR policy of your user names.
Name another unit as \fIunit\fR/\fIpolicy\fR.
Needs \fBadd:policy\fR, or \fBadd:policy:<type>\fR, on that unit.
.br
A new policy does nothing until it is assigned to an object, see \fBPolicy Assignment\fR.
.TP
.B del \fR[\fB\-\-no\-trash\fR] \fIpolicy\fR
Delete a policy.
It goes to the trash, from where \fBotpme-trash\fR(1) can bring it back with its assignments; \fB\-\-no\-trash\fR deletes it right away.
.br
The question names every object the policy is assigned to; \fB\-f\fR deletes without asking.
Needs \fBdelete:object\fR on the policy, or \fBdelete:policy\fR (or \fBdelete:policy:<type>\fR) on its unit.
.TP
.B show \fR[\fB\-a\fR] [\fB\-z\fR \fIN\fR] [\fB\-\-fields\fR \fIF1,F2\fR] [\fB\-\-limit\fR \fIN\fR] [\fB\-\-policy\-limit\fR \fIN\fR] [\fIpolicy\fR]
Without a policy, a table of all of them with their unit, type and status; the options below are for that table.
With a policy, its settings: what the commands of its type set, see \fBPOLICY TYPE DETAILS\fR.
.RS
.TP
.B \-a
Also the policies you may only look at, not just the ones you may change.
.TP
.B \-z \fIN\fR
How many policies to print.
.TP
.B \-\-fields \fIF1,F2\fR
Print these columns and no others.
The names are the ones in the header of the table: \fBpolicy\fR, \fBunit\fR, \fBtype\fR, \fBstatus\fR, \fBpolicies\fR, \fBinherit\fR, \fBdescription\fR.
.TP
.B \-\-limit \fIN\fR, \fB\-\-policy\-limit\fR \fIN\fR
How many entries to print in the column \fBpolicies\fR, the policies assigned to the policy itself.
5 by default, 30 when you show one policy.
.RE
.na
.nh
.TP
.B show_oid \fR[\fB\-\-full\fR] \fIpolicy\fR
Show the OID of the policy: \fB\%policy|<realm>/<site>/<name>\fR.
With \fB\-\-full\fR the full OID, which names the unit as well: \fB\%policy|<realm>/<site>/<unit_path>/<name>\fR.
.br
Needs the ACL \fBview_public:object\fR.
.TP
.B show_uuid \fIpolicy\fR
Show the UUID of the policy.
.br
Needs the ACL \fBview_public:object\fR.
.hy
.ad
.TP
.B list \fR[\fIpattern\fR]
List the policy names, and with a pattern only those that match it, see
.B SELECTING OBJECTS
in
.BR otpme (1).
.TP
.B enable \fIpolicy\fR
Enable a disabled policy.
.TP
.B disable \fIpolicy\fR
Disable a policy without deleting it.
A disabled policy does nothing: it stays assigned, but no check, default or ID range comes from it.
.br
A policy is never disabled by a time: it has no \fBauto_disable\fR, and an auto-disable policy is not for policies.
.TP
.B rename \fIpolicy\fR \fInew_name\fR
Rename a policy.
It asks first; \fB\-f\fR does not.
The assignments stay, they hold the UUID.
.TP
.B move \fR[\fB\-\-keep\-acls\fR] \fIpolicy\fR \fIunit\fR
Move the policy into another unit.
Needs \fBadd:policy\fR on the new unit.
Its ACLs move with it: the default ACLs of the old unit are taken away and those of the new one are applied.
\fB\-\-keep\-acls\fR leaves them as they are.
.SS Policy Configuration
What a policy does is set with the commands of its type, see \fBPOLICY TYPE DETAILS\fR.
\fBconfig\fR sets the parameters every object has; for a policy these are \fBchangelog\fR and \fBallow_changelog_clearing\fR, see \fBObject Changelog\fR.
.TP
.B config \fR[\fB\-d\fR] [\fB\-a\fR] \fIpolicy\fR \fIparameter\fR \fR[\fIvalue\fR]
Set a parameter on the policy.
.RS
.TP
.B \-d
Remove the parameter from the policy, or with a value only that one value from a list.
It is not set back to a default: what applies afterwards is whatever the unit or the site says.
.TP
.B \-a
Append the value to a parameter that holds a list, instead of replacing what is there.
.RE
.IP
The ACL is per parameter, \fBset:config:<parameter>\fR.
.TP
.B show_config \fIpolicy\fR \fR[\fIparameter\fR]
The parameters set on the policy itself, or only the one named.
Nothing is shown for a parameter that the policy gets from a unit or from the site.
.TP
.B get_config \fIpolicy\fR \fIparameter\fR
What the parameter comes to for this policy, once the policy, its units and its site have been looked at.
So this answers what applies, where \fBshow_config\fR answers what was set here.
.TP
.B description \fIpolicy\fR \fR[\fIdescription\fR]
Set the description.
Without one OTPme asks and offers the current text for editing.
Needs \fBedit:description\fR.
.TP
.B info \fR[\fB\-\-language\fR \fILANG\fR] \fIpolicy\fR \fR[\fIinfo\fR]
Set free-form info text.
If \fIinfo\fR is omitted, the current info text is opened in the editor named by the \fBEDITOR\fR environment variable.
Needs \fBedit:info\fR.
.br
Without
.B \-\-language
the text is the administrator's own note.
Nothing shows the info of a policy to a user, so a text for a language stays a note as well.
.TP
.B dump_info \fR[\fB\-\-language\fR \fILANG\fR] \fIpolicy\fR
Write one of those texts to stdout.
\fB\-\-language\fR picks which one; without it you get the note of the administrator.
Needs \fBview:info\fR.
.SS Policy Assignment
A policy can have policies assigned to it like any other object.
They act on the policy itself, not on the objects it is assigned to.
Two types can be assigned to a policy:
.RS
.IP \(bu 3
\fBauthonaction\fR: who changes the policy has to log in again first.
.IP \(bu 3
\fBautodisable\fR: the policy is disabled after the time set there, and from then on does nothing.
.RE
.PP
A \fIchild_policy\fR is the name of a policy, not a path.
It is looked for in the site of the policy it is assigned to.
.PP
Assigning and removing ask before they change anything; the global option \fB\-f\fR skips the question.
.TP
.B add_policy \fIpolicy\fR \fIchild_policy\fR
Assign a policy to this policy.
A type that is not for policies is refused, and so is a second policy of a type that allows only one per object.
.br
Needs the ACL \fBassign:policy\fR.
.TP
.B remove_policy \fIpolicy\fR \fIchild_policy\fR
Remove a policy from this policy.
.br
Needs the ACL \fBremove:policy\fR, and that one verbatim: a plain \fBremove\fR does not cover it.
Removing a policy takes away what it enforces, so it must not be cheaper than assigning one.
.TP
.B list_policies \fR[\fB\-\-return\-type\fR \fITYPE\fR] [\fB\-\-policy\-types\fR \fIt1,t2\fR] \fIpolicy\fR
List the policies assigned to this policy, disabled ones too, marked \fB(disabled)\fR.
\fB\-\-return\-type\fR picks what is printed: \fBname\fR (the default), \fBread_oid\fR, \fBfull_oid\fR or \fBuuid\fR.
\fB\-\-policy\-types\fR lists only policies of these types; they are policy types, not policy names.
.br
Needs the ACL \fBview:policies\fR.
.SS ACL Management
An ACL says what somebody may do with this policy.
The somebody is a role or a token, never a user.
.PP
What the three forms \fBacl\fR, \fB+acl\fR and \fB++acl\fR mean is under \fBACLs\fR in
.BR otpme (7).
Tab completion after \fBadd_acl\fR offers the ACLs a policy knows, those of \fBpolicy\fR and those of its type.
.PP
A command that changes something asks first; the global option \fB\-f\fR skips the question.
.TP
.B add_acl \fIpolicy\fR \fIowner_type\fR \fIowner\fR \fIacl\fR[:\fIvalue\fR][,\fIacl\fR[:\fIvalue\fR]]
Give a role or a token one or more ACLs on this policy.
.br
\fIowner_type\fR is \fBrole\fR or \fBtoken\fR.
\fIowner\fR names it the way \fBlist_acls\fR prints it: a role as \fIsite/name\fR, or by its name alone on its own site; a token as \fI\%user/token\fR.
An ACL can carry a value, for example \fBview:policy_type\fR.
To give several ACLs at once, separate them with commas: \fB\%view:policy_type,view:status\fR.
.br
Needs the ACL \fBadd:acl\fR.
.TP
.B del_acl \fIpolicy\fR \fIacl\fR[,\fIacl\fR]
Take those ACLs away again.
.br
Here an \fIacl\fR is the whole entry, owner and all, the way \fBlist_acls\fR prints it: \fBrole:berlin/pw\-admins:view:policy_type\fR.
.br
Needs the ACL \fBdelete:acl\fR.
.TP
.B list_acls \fIpolicy\fR
Show the ACLs that lie on this policy, i.e. who may do what with it.
Needs the ACL \fBview:acl\fR.
.TP
.B enable_acl_inheritance \fIpolicy\fR
Let a move of the policy swap its default ACLs: what the old unit handed down is taken away, and what the new unit hands down is given.
It does not by default; a move then leaves the ACLs of the policy as they are.
.RS
.PP
Only a move asks this.
A new policy gets the default ACLs of its unit, and \fBadd_acl \-a\fR or \fB\-r\fR on a unit reach it either way.
A move with \fB\-\-keep\-acls\fR leaves its ACLs alone all the same.
.PP
A move by somebody who does not hold \fBadd:acl\fR and \fBdelete:acl\fR on the policy leaves its ACLs alone as well, and says so.
Otherwise moving an object into a unit would be a way of handing yourself the default ACLs of that unit.
.PP
Needs the ACL \fBenable:acl_inheritance\fR.
.RE
.TP
.B disable_acl_inheritance \fIpolicy\fR
A move leaves the ACLs of the policy as they are again.
.br
Needs the ACL \fBdisable:acl_inheritance\fR.
.SS Object Changelog
Every change to the object writes an entry: who made it, when, and what was done.
A change can carry a comment of its own, through the global option \fB\-\-changelog\fR.
.PP
Unless the \fBchangelog\fR config parameter says otherwise.
It is read along the cascade of the policy, and nothing is written where it is off \(em not for that policy, and not for the ones below a unit that switched it off.
Without any value anywhere it is on.
.PP
An \fIentry_id\fR is the number the listing shows in front of an entry, counted from 0.
.PP
Only the comment of an entry can be changed or removed.
What was done and by whom stays as it is.
.PP
A command that changes something asks first; the global option \fB\-f\fR skips the question.
.TP
.B changelog \fIpolicy\fR
Show the changelog of the policy, one entry per line: its \fIentry_id\fR, the time, who made the change and what was done, then a detail in round brackets and the comment in square brackets where there are any.
Needs the ACL \fBview:changelog\fR.
.TP
.B edit_changelog \fIpolicy\fR \fIentry_id\fR \fIcomment\fR
Set the comment of that entry, or replace the one it has.
That writes an entry of its own.
Needs the ACL \fBedit:changelog\fR.
.TP
.B del_changelog \fIpolicy\fR \fIentry_id\fR
Remove the comment of that entry.
The entry itself stays, and an entry without a comment is refused.
That writes an entry of its own.
Needs the ACL \fBedit:changelog\fR.
.TP
.B clear_changelog \fIpolicy\fR
Remove every entry of the changelog.
Afterwards it holds one entry: that it was cleared.
.br
This needs more than the ACL \fBclear:changelog\fR: the config parameter
.B allow_changelog_clearing
has to allow it (see \fBotpme\fR(7)).
It is true by default, but where no value is found at all, clearing is refused.
A value set on the token you are signed in with counts first.
Without one, the value of the object counts, read along its cascade.
.SS Export and Cleanup
Both write to stdout.
There is no import on this page: reading an export back in is
.B otpme\-tool import\fR,
see
.BR otpme\-tool (1).
.PP
.B remove_orphans
asks before it changes anything; the global option \fB\-f\fR skips the question.
.TP
.B export \fR[\fB\-\-password\fR \fIpassword\fR] \fIpolicy\fR
Write the whole object of the policy to stdout, as JSON.
.RS
.PP
Without \fB\-\-password\fR it is written as it is, in clear.
With one, a key is derived from that password and the object is encrypted with it; the salt goes into the output, so the password is all you need to read it back.
.PP
What a policy names, the groups, roles, units, policies or templates of its type, it names by UUID, so an export says something only in the realm it came from.
.PP
Needs the ACL \fBexport\fR.
.RE
.TP
.B remove_orphans \fIpolicy\fR
Remove what the policy still points at although it is gone.
.RS
.PP
On every policy that is its ACLs and the policies assigned to it.
Its type adds its own lists: the units of \fBdefaultunits\fR, the roles of \fBdefaultroles\fR, the groups of \fBdefaultgroups\fR, the policies of \fBdefaultpolicies\fR, the whitelist of \fBauthonaction\fR, the templates of \fBobjecttemplates\fR and the dictionaries of \fBpassword\fR.
.PP
It shows everything it found and asks once for all of it.
It asks nothing and changes nothing when it finds nothing.
.PP
Needs the ACL \fBremove:orphans\fR.
.RE
.SH POLICY TYPE DETAILS
.SS Password Policy
Checks a password or a PIN when it is set: on a token, on a user, on a host or node.
A token without a password policy of its own is checked with the one of its user.
.PP
A new policy asks for at least 8 characters with a number, an upper- and a lowercase letter and a special character, a PIN of at least 4 digits, and a strength score of at least 2 from the checker \fBspsc\fR.
A PIN is digits only.
Which characters a password may contain at all is the config parameter \fBpassword_allowed_chars\fR of the site, unit or user.
.PP
The \fBenable_\fR and \fBdisable_\fR commands change at once; the others ask first, and \fB\-f\fR skips the question.
.RS
.TP
.B password_min_len \fIpolicy\fR \fIlength\fR
.TQ
.B pin_min_len \fIpolicy\fR \fIlength\fR
The shortest password, the shortest PIN.
Needs \fB\%edit:password_min_len\fR, \fB\%edit:pin_min_len\fR.
.TP
\fBenable_require_number\fR|\fB_upper\fR|\fB_lower\fR|\fB_special\fR \fIpolicy\fR
.TQ
\fBdisable_require_number\fR|\fB_upper\fR|\fB_lower\fR|\fB_special\fR \fIpolicy\fR
Whether a password needs a number, an uppercase letter, a lowercase letter, a special character.
Needs \fB\%enable:number\fR, \fB\%disable:number\fR, and the same with \fBuppercase\fR, \fBlowercase\fR, \fBspecial\fR.
.TP
.B enable_strength_checker \fIpolicy\fR
.TQ
.B disable_strength_checker \fIpolicy\fR
Whether the strength of a password is scored on top of the rules above.
Needs \fB\%enable:strength_checker\fR, \fB\%disable:strength_checker\fR.
.TP
.B strength_checker \fIpolicy\fR \fIchecker\fR
Which checker scores it.
\fBspsc\fR is the only one.
Needs \fB\%edit:strength_checker\fR.
.TP
.B strength_checker_opts \fIpolicy\fR \fIoptions\fR
The options of the checker, as \fIname\fR=\fIvalue\fR separated by a semicolon, e.g. \fB\(dqmin_score=3;dict_order=en,de\(dq\fR.
Each call replaces all of them; what is not given falls back to its default.
Needs \fB\%edit:strength_checker_opts\fR.
.RS
.TP
\fBmin_score\fR=\fIN\fR
The lowest score a password may have.
A score runs from 0 to 10 and is about the years it takes to guess the password at a million tries a second.
Default 2.
.TP
\fBdict_order\fR=\fId1\fR,\fId2\fR
The dictionaries a password is looked up in, and in which order; see
.BR otpme\-dictionary (1).
Default none.
A dictionary that is disabled or deleted is skipped.
The \fBpassword_strength\fR policy of a site has all the dictionaries the site was created with.
.TP
\fBrecent_years_past\fR=\fIN\fR, \fBrecent_years_future\fR=\fIN\fR
Years in a password that count as easy to guess, counted back and forward from this year.
Default 150 and 10.
.RE
.TP
.B test \fR[\fB\-\-password\fR \fIpassword\fR] [\fB\-\-pin\fR \fIpin\fR] \fIpolicy\fR
Check a password or a PIN against the policy, without setting it.
For a password it prints the score and whether it is strong enough.
.RE
.SS Login Times Policy
When a login is allowed.
It is checked at every login, on the token, the user and the access group, on the host, node or client, and on the roles that let the token in there.
An admin token is always let in.
The time is the local time of the node that checks the login.
.PP
A site has \fBworkhours_login\fR (\fB* 6\-18 * * 1\-5\fR, Monday to Friday 6:00 to 18:59) and \fBweekend_login\fR (\fB* * * * 6\-7\fR), assigned to nothing.
.RS
.TP
.B login_times \fIpolicy\fR \fItimes\fR
When a login is allowed, like the time fields of
.BR cron (5):
minute (0\-59), hour (0\-23), day of month (1\-31), month (1\-12), day of week (1\-7, Monday is 1).
A field is \fB*\fR, a number, a range like \fB8\-17\fR, or a list of them like \fB1,3,5\-7\fR.
A range whose end is below its start goes round: \fB22\-6\fR is 22:00 to 6:59.
Several entries are separated by \fB|\fR; a login is allowed if one of them matches, e.g. \fB\(dq* 8\-17 * * 1\-5|* 9\-12 * * 6\(dq\fR.
.br
It asks first; \fB\-f\fR does not.
Needs \fB\%edit:login_times\fR.
.TP
.B test \-\-object\-type \fItype\fB \-\-test\-object \fIobject\fB \-\-token \fItoken\fR \fIpolicy\fR
Check whether \fItoken\fR may log in now, with the policy as if it were assigned to \fIobject\fR of \fItype\fR.
\fIobject\fR is a name, for a token its path.
All three are required.
.RE
.SS Auto-Disable Policy
Disables the object it is assigned to once its time is up: when the object is loaded, and at the latest when the master node looks, every 5 minutes.
The time counts from when the policy was assigned.
.PP
To enable such an object again takes \fB\-f\fR, and the time starts anew.
.PP
A site has \fBauto_disable_hour\fR, \fB_day\fR, \fB_week\fR, \fB_month\fR and \fB_year\fR (+1h, +1D, +1W, +1M, +1Y), assigned to nothing.
.RS
.TP
.B auto_disable \fR[\fB\-u\fR] \fIpolicy\fR \fItime\fR
After how long, or when.
A relative time has a leading \fB+\fR, e.g. \fB+30D\fR for 30 days; the units are \fBs\fR, \fBm\fR, \fBh\fR, \fBD\fR, \fBW\fR, \fBM\fR (30 days) and \fBY\fR (365 days), see
.BR otpme (7).
An absolute one is a date and a time, e.g. \fB\(dq09:53 13.06.2027\(dq\fR or \fB\(dq2027\-06\-13 09:53\(dq\fR.
.br
With \fB\-u\fR the time counts from the last use of the object instead, so it is disabled once it was unused for that long.
That takes a relative time, and an object that keeps its last use, see \fBPOLICY TYPES\fR; one that was never used counts from the assignment.
.br
It asks first; \fB\-f\fR does not.
Needs \fB\%edit:auto_disable\fR.
.RE
.SS Force Token Policy
Which tokens may log in there.
It is checked at every login on the role, host, node, client or access group it is assigned to, for admin tokens too.
A link token is checked as the token it points to.
.PP
A token is let in when its type is in \fBforce_token_types\fR (if that is set) and its pass type in \fBforce_pass_types\fR (if that is set).
The pass types are \fBotp\fR (hotp, totp, motp), \fBotp_push\fR, \fBtiqr\fR, \fBsmartcard\fR (fido2, u2f, passkey, yubikey_hmac, yubikey_piv), \fBssh_key\fR, \fBstatic\fR (password, and an ssh token used with a password), and \fBscript_otp\fR and \fBscript_static\fR, which are the types of the script tokens themselves.
.PP
A new policy has no token types and the pass types otp, otp_push, tiqr, ssh_key and smartcard.
A site has \fBforce_token_otp\fR (otp, otp_push, tiqr) and \fBforce_token_non_static\fR (the same with ssh_key and smartcard), assigned to nothing.
.PP
Both commands take a comma separated list and replace the one there; \fB\(dq\(dq\fR empties it, so it no longer limits anything.
A type OTPme does not know is refused.
They ask first; \fB\-f\fR does not.
.RS
.TP
.B force_token_types \fIpolicy\fR \fItypes\fR
The token types let in, e.g. \fBtotp,fido2\fR.
Needs \fB\%edit:force_token_types\fR.
.TP
.B force_pass_types \fIpolicy\fR \fItypes\fR
The pass types let in, e.g. \fBotp,smartcard\fR.
Needs \fB\%edit:force_pass_types\fR.
.RE
.SS Auth On Action Policy
Asks the user to log in again before an action: the client gets a new JWT for the token the user is logged in with.
A site has \fBauth_on_action\fR, on every object.
.PP
It does not ask in API mode (directly on a node, without the daemons), over the local socket of a node, and not for the tokens and roles on its whitelist.
.PP
A hook is an action on an object type, e.g. \fBuser change_pin\fR or \fBunit pre_add_user\fR.
A new policy gets every hook there is.
\fBshow\fR \fIpolicy\fR lists the ones it has (\fBHOOKS\fR) and the ones removed by hand (\fBREMOVED_HOOKS\fR).
Every object type has these; a command of another name is in brackets:
.\" BEGIN COMMON HOOKS
.RS
.PP
.na
.nh
add, add_acl, add_attribute, add_extension, add_object_class, add_policy, change_auto_disable\ (auto_disable), change_description\ (description), change_info\ (info), change_unit\ (move), del_acl, del_attribute, del_object_class, delete\ (del), disable, disable_acl_inheritance, enable, enable_acl_inheritance, export, get_acls\ (list_acls), modify, remove_extension, remove_orphans, remove_policy, rename, show_config_parameters\ (show_config).
.hy
.ad
.RE
.\" END COMMON HOOKS
.PP
The hooks an object type has on top of these are in its man page, section \fBAUTH ON ACTION HOOKS\fR.
.PP
The commands ask first; \fB\-f\fR does not.
.RS
.TP
.B add_hook \fIpolicy\fR \fIobject_type\fR \fIhook\fR
.TQ
.B remove_hook \fIpolicy\fR \fIobject_type\fR \fIhook\fR
Ask, or no longer ask, before that action.
A hook OTPme does not know for the object type is refused.
A removed hook is remembered, so \fBupdate_hooks\fR does not bring it back; \fBadd_hook\fR does, and forgets the removal.
Needs \fBadd:hook\fR, \fBremove:hook\fR.
.TP
.B update_hooks \fIpolicy\fR
Add the hooks registered after the policy was added, e.g. by an OTPme update, except the ones removed by hand.
Needs \fBadd:hook\fR.
.TP
.B reauth_timeout \fIpolicy\fR \fItime\fR
How long one login again holds while the user keeps working: every action within it starts it anew.
Default 30s; 0 asks before every action.
A number of seconds, or with a unit like \fB5m\fR.
Needs \fB\%edit:reauth_timeout\fR.
.TP
.B reauth_expiry \fIpolicy\fR \fItime\fR
How long it holds at most, from the first login again, however busy the user is.
Default 0, no limit.
Has to be above \fBreauth_timeout\fR.
Needs \fB\%edit:reauth_expiry\fR.
.TP
.B whitelist_token \fIpolicy\fR \fIuser/token\fR
.TQ
.B unwhitelist_token \fIpolicy\fR \fIuser/token\fR
.TQ
.B whitelist_role \fIpolicy\fR \fIrole\fR
.TQ
.B unwhitelist_role \fIpolicy\fR \fIrole\fR
Tokens and roles that are never asked.
A token in a role counts, however deep the roles are nested.
The role is looked for in this site.
Needs \fB\%add:whitelist\fR, \fB\%remove:whitelist\fR.
.RE
.SS Default Units Policy
Says in which unit a new object goes when \fBadd\fR names no unit.
It is assigned to users and works for what that user adds.
Every new user gets \fBdefault_units\fR, which names the usual unit of each type, e.g. \fBusers\fR for a user and \fBhosts\fR for a host.
.PP
When a user adds an object without a unit, it goes to the unit that the policy of that user names for its type.
If the policy names none, or the unit is gone, it goes to the usual unit of the type.
The same holds for lines without a unit in \fBotpme\-tool mass_object_add\fR.
Hosts and nodes that join, and templates, always go to the usual unit.
.PP
A user has one defaultunits policy.
To give a user another one, remove \fBdefault_units\fR first.
\fBshow\fR \fIpolicy\fR lists the units as \fBDEFAULT_UNITS\fR.
.PP
Commands:
.RS
.TP
.B set_unit \fIpolicy\fR \fIobject_type\fR \fR[\fIunit\fR]
Set the unit for \fIobject_type\fR.
\fIunit\fR is the path below the site, e.g. \fB\%it/users\fR.
Without \fIunit\fR, the type has none again and goes to its usual unit.
\fIobject_type\fR is one of:
.na
.nh
accessgroup, ca, client, device, dictionary, group, host, node, pass, policy, pool, resolver, role, scope, script, share, user, vlan.
Needs \fB\%add:default_unit\fR to set a type the first time, \fB\%edit:default_unit\fR to change it, \fB\%remove:default_unit\fR to clear it.
.hy
.ad
.RE
.SS Default Groups Policy
Gives new users a default group and puts them into more groups.
It is assigned to units and works for users added to that unit; a unit above or the site does not count.
A new site has \fBdefault_groups\fR on the unit \fBusers\fR, with no groups in it, and every unit added later gets it too (from \fBdefault_policies\fR).
.PP
When a user is added without \fB\-\-group\fR and without a template that names a group, the user gets the default group of the policy.
Without one, it is the group named like the site, e.g. \fBberlin\fR on the site \fBberlin\fR (\fBroot\fR for the admin user).
.PP
When a user is added without \fB\-\-groups\fR and gets a default token, that token is added to each group of the policy; groups hold tokens, not users.
With \fB\-\-no\-default\-token\fR nothing is added.
.PP
Who assigns the policy to a unit needs the same rights on its groups as for setting them (see below).
\fBshow\fR \fIpolicy\fR lists them as \fBDEFAULT_GROUP\fR and \fBDEFAULT_GROUPS\fR.
.PP
Commands:
.RS
.TP
.B add_group \fIpolicy\fR \fIgroup\fR
Add a group.
Needs \fB\%add:default_group\fR on the policy and \fB\%add:token\fR on the group.
.TP
.B remove_group \fIpolicy\fR \fIgroup\fR
Remove a group.
Needs \fB\%remove:default_group\fR.
.TP
.B default_group \fIpolicy\fR \fIgroup\fR
Set the default group.
\fB\(dq\(dq\fR as \fIgroup\fR removes it.
.na
Needs \fB\%edit:default_group\fR on the policy and \fB\%add:default_group_user\fR on the group.
.ad
.RE
.SS Default Roles Policy
Puts the default token of new users into roles.
It is assigned to units and works for users added to that unit; a unit above or the site does not count.
A new site has \fBdefault_roles\fR on the unit \fBusers\fR, with the role \fBREALM_USER\fR in it, and every unit added later gets it too (from \fBdefault_policies\fR).
.PP
When a user is added and gets a default token, that token is added to each role of the policy.
\fB\-\-roles\fR adds its roles on top.
\fB\-\-role\fR turns the policy off for this user: the token goes to that role only, also not to \fBREALM_USER\fR.
With \fB\-\-no\-default\-token\fR nothing is added.
.PP
Who assigns the policy to a unit needs \fB\%add:token\fR on each of its roles.
\fBshow\fR \fIpolicy\fR lists them as \fBDEFAULT_ROLES\fR.
.PP
Commands:
.RS
.TP
.B add_default_role \fIpolicy\fR \fIrole\fR
Add a role.
Needs \fB\%add:default_role\fR on the policy and \fB\%add:token\fR on the role.
.TP
.B remove_default_role \fIpolicy\fR \fIrole\fR
Remove a role.
Needs \fB\%remove:default_role\fR.
.RE
.SS Default Policies Policy
Says which policies a new object gets.
It is assigned to sites and units.
A new object gets them from the unit it is added to, a unit right below the site from the site, and a token from the unit of its user.
A unit further up does not count.
.PP
The policy lists policies per object type.
A new object gets the ones listed for its type, without a check of rights; if one cannot be assigned, the object is not added.
Entries for site and realm do nothing: those get their policies when they are made.
.PP
A new site has \fBdefault_policies\fR on the site and on every unit, and it lists itself for units, so every unit added later has it too.
It lists \fBauth_on_action\fR for every type; \fBpassword_strength\fR for host, node, token and user; \fBdefault_units\fR and \fBtoken_acls\fR for user; and \fBdefault_groups\fR, \fBdefault_roles\fR, \fBobject_templates\fR and \fBdefault_policies\fR for unit.
.PP
.na
Who assigns the policy needs what assigning each of its policies needs.
\fBshow\fR \fIpolicy\fR lists them as \fBDEFAULT_POLICIES\fR, e.g. \fB\%user:[auth_on_action,default_units]\fR.
.ad
.PP
Commands:
.RS
.TP
.B add_default_policy \fIpolicy\fR \fIobject_type\fR \fIpolicy_name\fR
Add \fIpolicy_name\fR for \fIobject_type\fR.
It must be a policy of this site that can be assigned to that type.
Needs \fB\%add:default_policy\fR, and what assigning \fIpolicy_name\fR needs, e.g. \fB\%add:token\fR on each role of a defaultroles policy.
.TP
.B remove_default_policy \fIpolicy\fR \fIobject_type\fR \fIpolicy_name\fR
Remove it.
Needs \fB\%remove:default_policy\fR.
.RE
.SS ID Range Policy
.na
Says which uidNumber and gidNumber a new user or group gets.
A user or group added without one gets the next free number from the policy of its unit; if the unit has none, or none for that attribute, from the policy of the site.
A unit further up does not count.
A number given by hand must lie in a range too.
.PP
A new site has \fBid_range\fR with \fB\%uidNumber:s:70000\-80000\fR and \fB\%gidNumber:s:70000\-80000\fR, or the ranges given with \fB\-\-id\-ranges\fR.
Ranges must not overlap with any range of any idrange policy, also of other sites.
.PP
A range is written \fI<attribute>:<type>:<start>\-<end>\fR, e.g. \fB\%uidNumber:s:70000\-80000\fR.
\fIattribute\fR is \fBuidNumber\fR or \fBgidNumber\fR.
Type \fBs\fR hands out the numbers in order, going on after the last one handed out; at the end of the range it starts over.
Type \fBr\fR takes a free number at random.
Several ranges are separated by commas.
.PP
\fBshow\fR \fIpolicy\fR lists \fBID_RANGES\fR, \fBVERIFY_NEW_ID\fR and \fBRECHECK_ID_RANGES\fR.
.PP
Commands:
.RS
.TP
.B add_id_range \fIpolicy\fR \fIrange,...\fR
Add ranges.
If one of them is wrong or overlaps, none is added.
Needs \fB\%add:idrange\fR.
.TP
.B del_id_range \fIpolicy\fR \fIrange\fR
Remove a range.
Needs \fB\%delete:idrange\fR.
.TP
\fBenable_id_check\fR \fIpolicy\fR
.TQ
\fBdisable_id_check\fR \fIpolicy\fR
Before a number is handed out, check that no user or group has it.
On by default.
Without it, type \fBs\fR takes the number after the last one handed out unchecked.
Needs \fB\%enable:id_check\fR or \fB\%disable:id_check\fR.
.TP
\fBenable_id_range_recheck\fR \fIpolicy\fR
.TQ
\fBdisable_id_range_recheck\fR \fIpolicy\fR
When a range of type \fBs\fR has no free number after the last one handed out, look for gaps from its start.
Off by default.
Needs \fB\%enable:id_range_recheck\fR or \fB\%disable:id_range_recheck\fR.
.RE
.ad
.SS Token ACLs Policy
.na
.nh
Gives a new token its ACLs.
It is assigned to users; every new user gets \fBtoken_acls\fR.
When a token is added to the user, the policy adds:
.RS
.TP
.B user ACLs
on the user, for the new token: what a token may do with its user.
.TP
.B token ACLs
on the new token, for the token itself and for the default token of the user.
.TP
.B creator ACLs
on the new token, for the token that added it, when that one belongs to another user (e.g. an admin).
.RE
.PP
A token type can bring more of its own.
A token gets the ones its type knows, the others are left out.
When a token is deleted, the same ones are taken away.
When a token is moved to another user, the user and token ACLs of the old user's policy are taken away and those of the new user's policy are given, whatever ACL inheritance and \fB\-\-keep\-acls\fR say; the creator ACLs stay.
The user \fBTOKENSTORE\fR gets no user and token ACLs.
A change to the policy counts for tokens added or deleted after it.
.PP
A new site has \fBtoken_acls\fR with the user ACLs \fB\%view_all\fR, \fB\%gen_keys\fR, \fB\%encrypt\fR, \fB\%decrypt\fR and \fB\%sign\fR; the token ACLs \fB\%view\fR, \fB\%edit:pin\fR, \fB\%edit:password\fR and \fB\%delete\fR; and the creator ACLs \fB\%view\fR, \fB\%edit\fR and \fB\%delete\fR.
.PP
\fBshow\fR \fIpolicy\fR lists them as \fBUSER_ACLS\fR, \fBTOKEN_ACLS\fR and \fBTOKEN_CREATOR_ACLS\fR.
.PP
Commands:
.RS
.TP
\fBadd_user_acl\fR \fIpolicy\fR \fIacl\fR
.TQ
\fBdel_user_acl\fR \fIpolicy\fR \fIacl\fR
Add or remove a user ACL.
It must be an ACL of users.
Needs \fB\%add:user_acl\fR or \fB\%del:user_acl\fR.
.TP
\fBadd_token_acl\fR \fIpolicy\fR \fIacl\fR
.TQ
\fBdel_token_acl\fR \fIpolicy\fR \fIacl\fR
Add or remove a token ACL.
It must be an ACL of some token type.
Needs \fB\%add:token_acl\fR or \fB\%del:token_acl\fR.
.TP
\fBadd_creator_acl\fR \fIpolicy\fR \fIacl\fR
.TQ
\fBdel_creator_acl\fR \fIpolicy\fR \fIacl\fR
Add or remove a creator ACL.
It must be an ACL of some token type.
Needs \fB\%add:creator_acl\fR or \fB\%del:creator_acl\fR.
.RE
.hy
.ad
.SS Object Templates Policy
.na
.nh
Says which template a new user or host is made from.
It is assigned to units; every new unit gets \fBobject_templates\fR.
When a user or host is added to the unit, it gets the template the policy has for its type.
.PP
From its template, a user or host gets the policies, extensions, object classes and attributes, and whether ACL inheritance is on.
A user also gets the group of the template, if none is given; its key, agent, login and auth script and whether they are enabled; and autosign.
Templates get no template, nor do admins and base or internal users.
A template given with \fBotpme\-user add \-\-template\fR or \fBotpme\-host add \-\-template\fR wins over the policy.
\fBotpme\-user add \-\-no\-template\fR and \fBotpme\-host add \-\-no\-template\fR add one without any template.
.PP
A new user or host is made from the template of the policy without a check of rights, and a new user gets the group of the template.
So who sets a template, or assigns the policy to a unit, needs \fBuse\fR on the template.
\fB\-\-template\fR needs \fBuse\fR as well.
.PP
A new site has the templates \fBuser_template\fR and \fBhost_template\fR in the unit \fBtemplates\fR, and \fBobject_templates\fR with them.
.PP
\fBshow\fR \fIpolicy\fR lists them as \fBOBJECT_TEMPLATES\fR.
.PP
Commands:
.RS
.TP
\fBset_template\fR \fIpolicy\fR \fIobject_type\fR {\fItemplate\fR|\fB\(dq\(dq\fR}
Set the template for \fBuser\fR or \fBhost\fR.
It must be a template of the site, e.g. one added with \fBotpme\-user add \-t\fR.
\fB\(dq\(dq\fR removes it.
Needs \fB\%set_template\fR, and \fBuse\fR on the template.
.RE
.hy
.ad
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-type \fITYPE\fR
.na
.nh
The policy type.
Needed for \fBadd\fR and for every command in \fBPOLICY TYPE DETAILS\fR, e.g. \fBotpme\-policy \-\-type idrange add_id_range id_range \%uidNumber:s:80001\-89999\fR.
It can stand anywhere on the command line.
.hy
.ad
.PP
The options of each command are described with that command above.
.PP
Global options are available for all commands. See
.BR otpme (1)
for details.
.SH CONFIG PARAMETERS
.na
.nh
A policy takes these.
What each of them does, and how a parameter is resolved, is in
.BR otpme (7).
The settings of a policy type are no config parameters, they are made with the commands of the type, see \fBPOLICY TYPE DETAILS\fR.
.TP
.B changelog \fR(bool, default: true)
Write the commands that change the policy into its changelog, see \fBObject Changelog\fR.
.TP
.B allow_changelog_clearing \fR(bool, default: true)
Allow \fBclear_changelog\fR on the policy.
.hy
.ad
.SH EXAMPLES
.na
.nh
.SS Password Policy
.TP
.B otpme-policy \-\-type password add strong_pw
A new password policy already asks for upper- and lowercase, a number, a special character and a strength check.
.TP
.B otpme-policy \-\-type password password_min_len strong_pw 12
At least 12 characters.
.TP
.B otpme-policy \-\-type password pin_min_len strong_pw 6
PINs of at least 6 digits.
.TP
.B otpme-policy \-\-type password test \-\-password \(dqMyP@ss123\(dq strong_pw
Try a password against it.
.TP
.B otpme-user remove_policy alice password_strength
.TQ
.B otpme-user add_policy alice strong_pw
Give it to alice instead of the one of the site: a user has one password policy.
.SS Login Time Restrictions
.TP
.B otpme-policy \-\-type logintimes add office
.TQ
.B otpme-policy \-\-type logintimes login_times office \(dq*\ 8\-17\ *\ *\ 1\-5\(dq
Monday to Friday, 8:00 to 17:59.
.TP
.B otpme-accessgroup add_policy vpn office
Logins through the access group vpn only then.
.SS Auto-Disable Temporary Access
.TP
.B otpme-policy \-\-type autodisable add temp_access
.TQ
.B otpme-policy \-\-type autodisable auto_disable temp_access +90D
Disable after 90 days.
.TP
.B otpme-policy \-\-type autodisable auto_disable \-u temp_access +30D
Or instead: disable after 30 days without use.
.TP
.B otpme-user add_policy contractor1 temp_access
Give it to contractor1.
.SS Force Two-Factor Authentication
.TP
.B otpme-policy \-\-type forcetoken add require_2fa
.TQ
.B otpme-policy \-\-type forcetoken force_token_types require_2fa totp,fido2
Only TOTP and FIDO2 tokens.
.TP
.B otpme-accessgroup add_policy vpn require_2fa
Logins through the access group vpn only with those.
.SS Protect Sensitive Operations
.TP
.B otpme-policy \-\-type authonaction add protect_ops
A new one asks before every action there is.
.TP
.B otpme-policy \-\-type authonaction reauth_timeout protect_ops 5m
One login again holds 5 minutes of work.
.TP
.B otpme-policy \-\-type authonaction whitelist_role protect_ops SITE_ADMIN
Admins are never asked.
.TP
.B otpme-user remove_policy alice auth_on_action
.TQ
.B otpme-user add_policy alice protect_ops
Give it to alice instead of the one of the site.
.SS Units for New Objects
.TP
.B otpme-policy \-\-type defaultunits add it_units
.TQ
.B otpme-policy \-\-type defaultunits set_unit it_units user it/users
.TQ
.B otpme-policy \-\-type defaultunits set_unit it_units host it/servers
Users and hosts go to these units.
.TP
.B otpme-user remove_policy alice default_units
.TQ
.B otpme-user add_policy alice it_units
For what alice adds without naming a unit.
.SS ID Ranges
.TP
.B otpme-policy \-\-type idrange add it_ids
.TQ
.B otpme-policy \-\-type idrange add_id_range it_ids \%uidNumber:s:10000\-60000
User IDs from 10000 to 60000, in order.
.TP
.B otpme-unit add_policy it it_ids
New users in the unit it get theirs from here.
.hy
.ad
.\" BEGIN AUTH ON ACTION HOOKS
.\" Generated by update_hooks_man.py. Do not edit, the next run
.\" overwrites it.
.SH AUTH ON ACTION HOOKS
An auth on action policy can ask for a login again before these actions.
See
.B Auth On Action Policy
above.
A policy has the hooks every object type has, and these; a command of another name is in brackets:
.RS
.PP
.na
.nh
add_creator_acl, add_default_group, add_default_policy, add_default_role, add_hook, add_id_range, add_token_acl, add_user_acl, add_whitelist\ (whitelist_role or whitelist_token), change_default_group\ (default_group), change_force_pass_types\ (force_pass_types), change_force_token_types\ (force_token_types), change_login_times\ (login_times), change_password_min_len\ (password_min_len), change_pin_min_len\ (pin_min_len), change_reauth_expiry\ (reauth_expiry), change_reauth_timeout\ (reauth_timeout), change_strength_checker\ (strength_checker), change_strength_checker_opts\ (strength_checker_opts), del_creator_acl, del_id_range, del_token_acl, del_user_acl, disable_require_lower, disable_require_number, disable_require_special, disable_require_upper, disable_strength_checker, enable_require_lower, enable_require_number, enable_require_special, enable_require_upper, enable_strength_checker, remove_default_group, remove_default_policy, remove_default_role, remove_hook, remove_whitelist\ (unwhitelist_role or unwhitelist_token), set_config_parameter\ (config), set_default_unit\ (set_unit), set_template, update_hooks.
.hy
.ad
.RE
.\" END AUTH ON ACTION HOOKS
.SH FILES
.na
.nh
.TP
\fI/var/lib/otpme/tree/\fR
The objects of the realm, on the nodes and, by sync, on the hosts.
A policy is the directory \fI<policy>.policy\fR in the directory of its unit, e.g. \fI<realm>.realm/<site>.site/policies.unit/password_strength.policy/\fR, with its settings in \fIobject.json\fR.
Change them with the commands above, not in the file.
.hy
